netlab (2.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Intercepter mode: NetLab is no longer passive-only.
  * New Passwords page. Credentials and hashes are extracted from FTP,
    Telnet, POP3, IMAP, SMTP (SASL PLAIN/LOGIN), NNTP, IRC, Redis, SOCKS5,
    rlogin, LDAP simple bind, SNMP communities, MSSQL TDS7, MySQL,
    PostgreSQL, VNC, Kerberos AS-REQ pre-auth, HTTP Basic/Digest/Bearer and
    NTLMSSP in every carrier it appears in. Hashes are emitted in hashcat
    and john formats with the mode attached, and can be exported.
  * New Interception page: engagement scope, module selection, arming with
    an explicit confirmation, live status and an event log.
  * Active modules: full-duplex ARP poisoning with guaranteed restore,
    SSL strip, TLS interception with a locally generated CA, DNS spoofing
    with real forwarding for everything unmatched, rogue DHCP, traffic
    changer, cookie killer, file carving and a promiscuous-host probe.
  * New Files page carves readable response bodies to the engagement
    directory, hashed and typed as the server declared them.
  * New Rules page edits traffic-changer and DNS-spoof rules live.
  * New privileged helper, netlab-helper, started through pkexec over a
    pipe. Everything needing CAP_NET_RAW or CAP_NET_ADMIN happens there and
    nowhere else; the GUI stays unprivileged. The helper restores ARP,
    forwarding and nftables on disarm, on signal, on stdin EOF and at exit.
  * Every active action is gated on an on-disk engagement and written to a
    JSONL audit log.
  * HTTP credential headers are retained only while harvesting is enabled;
    the passive default still records presence and discards the value.
  * Added an opt-in body-data hook to the HTTP stream parser. Bodies are
    still counted and discarded unless a caller asks for them.

